# MalloySim scenario: a chain of masses on springs.
#
# Five bodies in a row, each linked to the next by a lightly damped spring, with
# the chain pulled taut at the start. It demonstrates the M13 capability: a
# many-to-one force pipeline, where the three interior bodies are each an
# endpoint of two springs and receive both contributions.
#
# The invariant to watch is the momentum columns. Every spring contributes equal
# and opposite endpoint forces, so a spring network cannot change total
# momentum, at any stiffness and any damping. Both columns hold flat for the
# whole run while the chain oscillates.
#
# Energy is a different story, and the columns show why it is split. KE and PE
# trade back and forth as the chain oscillates, while their sum decays because
# the springs are damped. Set every damping to 0.0 and the sum instead holds
# steady, bounded by the integrator's own drift rather than exactly constant.
#
# Springs are evaluated in the order declared here, which is part of the
# observable behaviour: floating-point addition is not associative, so a
# different order gives different low-order digits
# (docs/decisions/0008-spring-world-is-a-local-composition-boundary.md).

type springs

dt 0.0005
steps 20000
output_every 2500

# spring_body <mass> <px> <py> <vx> <vy>
spring_body 1.00  -3.00   0.00    0.00   0.35
spring_body 1.50  -1.40   0.20    0.00  -0.20
spring_body 0.75   0.30  -0.15    0.00   0.30
spring_body 2.00   1.90   0.25    0.00  -0.15
spring_body 1.25   3.40  -0.10    0.00   0.10

# spring <a> <b> <rest_length> <stiffness> <damping>
spring 0 1  1.20  45.0  0.30
spring 1 2  1.20  45.0  0.30
spring 2 3  1.20  45.0  0.30
spring 3 4  1.20  45.0  0.30

# Expected: momentum y holds at exactly 1.00000000e-01 for every report. Momentum
# x starts at exactly zero and stays at rounding level, around 1e-14, rather than
# printing a clean zero: the bodies are not symmetric, so the equal and opposite
# contributions cancel to within floating-point rounding rather than bit
# exactly. KE and PE trade back and forth while their sum decays from about
# 1.76e+01 to 6.9e+00 over the run, which is the damping doing its job.

# Machine-checkable assertions, evaluated against a real run by the scenario
# tests (issue #14). `step 0` is the state before any step, matching the column
# the app prints, and the tolerance is absolute.
# check step 0    kinetic     1.53750000e-01   tol 1e-9
# check step 0    elastic     1.74298603e+01   tol 1e-7
# check step 0    momentum_y  1.00000000e-01   tol 1e-12
# check step 20000 momentum_y 1.00000000e-01   tol 1e-12
